<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jehu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned c. 841–814 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jehu was king of Israel from about 841 to 814 BCE. An army commander, he seized the throne in a bloody coup supported by the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisha">Elisha</a>, killing King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoram-of-israel">Joram of Israel</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaziah-of-judah">Ahaziah of Judah</a> and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezebel">Jezebel</a> and wiping out the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-obelisk-of-shalmaneser-iii">Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III</a> shows him, or his envoy, bowing before the Assyrian king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jehu.jpg" alt="Jehu" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steven G. Johnson · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jehu_on_the_Black_Obelisk_of_Shalmaneser_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jehu was the tenth king of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeroboam-i">Jeroboam I</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoshaphat">Jehoshaphat</a> and grandson of Nimshi, and possibly a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a>, although the biblical text does not support the last claim. The main source for his reign is 2 Kings, which gives it as 28 years; scholars date it slightly differently, by about a year. Jehu was a military commander at the time of the Battle of Ramoth-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilead">Gilead</a> against the Arameans, in which King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoram-of-israel">Jehoram of Israel</a> was wounded and withdrew to Jezreel, attended by his nephew Ahaziah, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to Kings, the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisha">Elisha</a> sent one of his students to anoint Jehu as the agent of divine judgment against the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>. Jehu at first dismissed the young man as a madman, but his companions blew trumpets and proclaimed him king. At Jezreel he shot Jehoram with an arrow, and Ahaziah died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a> of his wounds. Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezebel">Jezebel</a> was thrown from a palace window at his command. At his order the chief men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> killed the 70 royal princes, and he killed 42 relatives of Ahaziah at Beth-eked. He then trapped and killed the worshippers and priests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>.</p><p>Little else is known of his reign apart from his tolerance of the golden calves at Dan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethel">Bethel</a>, which was criticised, and the pressure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael">Hazael</a>, king of the Arameans, who defeated his armies beyond the Jordan River. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-obelisk-of-shalmaneser-iii">Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III</a> depicts Jehu kissing the ground before the Assyrian king and records his tribute of much <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, dated about 841 BCE; it is the earliest preserved depiction of an Israelite. It calls him the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a>, which may reflect the Assyrian name for Israel as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omride-dynasty">House of Omri</a>. The Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosea">Hosea</a> says God would punish the house of Jehu for the massacre at Jezreel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jehu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Assyrian scribes went on calling Israel ‘the House of Omri’ for well over a century, long after <b>Jehu</b> had wiped out Omri’s dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.28,35.19&amp;year=-824" aria-label="Samaria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5455 990 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jehu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5905 1260h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5905 1260h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.28,35.19&amp;year=-824">What was here in 824 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/jehoahaz-of-israel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jehoahaz-of-israel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Jehoahaz of Israel</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; late 9th century BCE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;What hast thou to do with peace? turn thee behind me.&#8221;</p><footer>2 Kings 9:18, to the king's horseman who rode out to meet him &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as the Second Book of Kings tells it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/2_Kings" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
